==Problem==

A circle and two distinct lines are drawn on a sheet of paper. What is the largest possible number of points of intersection of these figures?

<imath>\textbf {(A)}\ 2 \qquad \textbf {(B)}\ 3 \qquad \textbf{(C)}\ 4 \qquad \textbf{(D)}\ 5 \qquad \textbf{(E)}\ 6</imath>

==Solution==

Each line may intersect the circle at most twice, while the two lines may intersect each other at most once.  This yields a total of <imath>2\cdot 2 + 1 = \boxed{\textbf{(D)}\ 5}</imath> intersections.

A possible configuration is shown below:

<asy>
size(60);
pair O=(0,0);
pair A=(1,1);
pair B=(-1,-1);
pair C=(1,-1);
pair D=(-1,1);
draw(Circle(O,1));
draw(A--B);
draw(C--D);
dot(O);
dot(A * sqrt(2)/2);
dot(B * sqrt(2)/2);
dot(C * sqrt(2)/2); 
dot(D * sqrt(2)/2);
</asy>

